    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a massive plateau with their back squat. they haven't added weight in 3 weeks and rpe is hitting 10 on almost every working set. can u explain how deloading or messing with volume might help them break through this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Deloadering (reducing training volume and intensity) and adjusting volume are common strategies to help break through a plateau. Reducing the load or increasing rest time may help your client's nervous system tolerate the weight they're lifting, allowing them to lift heavier and approach a new plateau. Conversely, a strategically managed block of higher volume can also help stimulate muscle growth by allowing for new neural adaptations and adversity training.",
